    "chunk_text": "I asked a relevant question whether there is a tax evasion on that basis. If cryptocurrency is not accepted in the country, then it is illegal. If it is illegal, then a tax evasion takes place. So, how is it dealt with from there onwards? And, the Answer that was given, Sir, is a very clear sign that the stable door had been closed after the horse had bolted. Therefore, I would urge you all, as a Government, please take a decision as to how the Central Bank should respond to matters of this nature. There are three areas which basically make it illegal, and you were saying that they are conducting seminars.\n\n\u0d9c\u0dd4 (\u0d86\u0da0\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dba) \u0d85\u0dba\u0dcf \u0dc3 \u0d9c\u0dba\u0dca\u0dad \u0dd9\u0dc4\u0dc3\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bc8\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bc8\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 (\u0b95\u0bc8\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbf\u0ba4\u0bbf) \u0b85\u0ba9\u0bbf\u0bb2\u0bcd \u0b9c \u0ba8\u0bcd\u0ba4) (The Hon. (Dr.) Anil Jayantha) Thank you very much.\n\nIt is very clearly mentioned in the Answer. I do not know the litigation that you just highlighted in that regard. But, with respect to cryptocurrencies, it was very clear mentioned that Sri Lankan Rupee is the legal tender and that others are not allowed. As of now, trading or exchanges in cryptocurrency are not allowed and are not recognized."
