    "description": "Hansard of 2026-07-09",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23956_english_2026-07-09.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-09-2026-07-09-0175",
    "chunk_index": 175,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "1c2157e4ef6db7ffc6b90553e08f2aa0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1794,
    "chunk_text": "But, Sir, that is not the issue before us today. The issue before this House is whether these Bills genuinely implement the FATF standards or whether the FATF is being used as a justification for granting powers that the FATF itself neither requires nor recommends. That distinction is fundamental. The Government repeatedly tells us that these measures are necessary because the FATF demands them. Yet, when one examines Recommendation 8 of the FATF, one discovers something very different. The FATF does not require governments to treat every civil society organization as a potential security threat. It does not recommend blanket restrictions; it does not advocate arbitrary surveillance or unnecessary interference with legitimate organizations. Instead, the FATF repeatedly states that measures must be targeted, proportionate and risk-based. It recognizes that many non-profit organizations are inherently low risk and encourages governments to work with the civil society, not against it.\n\nThe Government\u2019s own risk assessment reportedly concludes that the overall terrorist financing risk within the non-profit sector is low to medium. Yet organizations engaged in human rights work continue to face heightened scrutiny without objective evidence that they have engaged in terrorist financing or money laundering. That is not evidence-based regulation; it is regulation based on suspicion.\n\nThe consequences are already being felt. Nearly 70 per cent of the civil society organizations surveyed reported difficulties in receiving foreign funding, while half experienced difficulties in opening bank accounts. Projects have been delayed; staff salaries have gone unpaid; humanitarian programmes have been disrupted and communities have been denied services that they desperately need."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-09-2026-07-09",
    "num": "2026-07-09",
    "date_str": "2026-07-09",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-07-09",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23956_english_2026-07-09.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-09-2026-07-09-0176",
    "chunk_index": 176,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "cf03e2c010861ba012ed17cbd2728a7d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1842,
    "chunk_text": "The consequences are already being felt. Nearly 70 per cent of the civil society organizations surveyed reported difficulties in receiving foreign funding, while half experienced difficulties in opening bank accounts. Projects have been delayed; staff salaries have gone unpaid; humanitarian programmes have been disrupted and communities have been denied services that they desperately need.\n\nConsider a small money exchange business in Pettah. Under the proposed Amendments, the owner may face penalties up to Rs. 100 million for failing to comply with transaction thresholds that are not even contained in the Act itself, but will be determined later through rules issued by the Head of the Financial Intelligence Unit. Parliament is being asked to approve offences today, while leaving the substance of the law to be determined tomorrow through administrative directions. That is not how legislation should be enacted in a constitutional democracy.\n\nConsider a rural microfinance institution serving women-led households and small farmers. Many of its customers may lack complete documentation because of poverty or natural disasters. Faced with uncertain legal obligations, the institution may simply refuse loans to those who need them most rather than risking severe penalties. The result is, the burden of regulatory uncertainty falls not on criminals, but on the poorest members of the society.\n\nConsider a civil society organization that receives funding from an international donor to rebuilt a school. If the transfer is delayed because it is automatically treated with suspicion, construction would be postponed, contractors would remain unpaid, teachers cannot be compensated and the vulnerable children lose access to education. Once again, those who suffer are not criminals, but ordinary citizens.\n\n501 502\n\n2026 \u0da2\u0dd6\u0dd2 09"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-09-2026-07-09",
    "num": "2026-07-09",
    "date_str": "2026-07-09",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-07-09",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23956_english_2026-07-09.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-09-2026-07-09-0177",
    "chunk_index": 177,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "1de2f1f8970a9ea5bcbc2d8e5a701cc7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1692,
    "chunk_text": "Consider a civil society organization that receives funding from an international donor to rebuilt a school. If the transfer is delayed because it is automatically treated with suspicion, construction would be postponed, contractors would remain unpaid, teachers cannot be compensated and the vulnerable children lose access to education. Once again, those who suffer are not criminals, but ordinary citizens.\n\n501 502\n\n2026 \u0da2\u0dd6\u0dd2 09\n\nOr, consider a father whose bank accounts are frozen while he is merely under investigation. He may not have been even charged or convicted yet, but his family cannot pay rent, purchase food, obtain medicine or meet children's school expenses. To access his own money, he must first embark upon lengthy and expensive legal proceedings while being deprived of the very resources needed to defend himself.\n\nSir, those examples illustrate a common theme - the Bills before us significantly expand the executive discretion while reducing meaningful safeguards. They permit broader administrative powers; they diminish judicial oversight; they widen information sharing; they permit intrusive investigative techniques; they extend powers to freeze financial assets and increasingly replace clear statutory standards with future rules and administrative directions.\n\nThe Government presents these measures as unavoidable because the FATF requires them. But, where does the FATF require Parliament to surrender its legislative responsibility, where does the FATF require broader executive discretion without adequate judicial oversight and where does the FATF require restrictions that are neither targeted nor proportionate? The answer is simple, it does not."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-09-2026-07-09",
    "num": "2026-07-09",
    "date_str": "2026-07-09",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-07-09",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23956_english_2026-07-09.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-09-2026-07-09-0178",
    "chunk_index": 178,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "df90c135b0555017b2fbc071685e311f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2054,
    "chunk_text": "The Government presents these measures as unavoidable because the FATF requires them. But, where does the FATF require Parliament to surrender its legislative responsibility, where does the FATF require broader executive discretion without adequate judicial oversight and where does the FATF require restrictions that are neither targeted nor proportionate? The answer is simple, it does not.\n\nHistory teaches us that powers granted in the name of security rarely remain confined to their original purpose. Today, these powers may be directed against civil society organizations; tomorrow, they may be directed against journalists and the day after, they may be directed against businesses. Ultimately, every citizen becomes vulnerable when executive discretion replaces judicial oversight and due process. That is why this House must examine these Bills with the utmost care. No one in this House is asking that Sri Lanka abandon FATF compliance. On the contrary, we should fully comply with the FATF. But, let us comply with FATF as FATF itself intended through evidence-based, proportionate and risk-based regulation. Let us not invoke the FATF as a justification for expanding executive power beyond what FATF itself\n\nrequires. National security and constitutional liberty are not competing values. A democratic State worthy of the name protects both.\n\nI, therefore, urge this House to reconsider these Bills to ensure that they faithfully reflect the FATF\u2019s own standards, strengthen rather than weaken judicial oversight and preserve the constitutional rights and democratic freedoms that Parliament has a solemn duty to protect.\n\nI thank you, Sir. \u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0db8\u0dd6\u0dbd\u0dcf\u0dca\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dd6\u0daa \u0dd9\u0dca\u0dd3\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0dd9\u0dcf (\u0bc8\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bc8\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0ba4\u0b95\u0bc8\u0b95\u0bc8\u0ba4\u0bbe\u0b99\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd \u0b89\u0bb1\u0bc1\u0baa\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bbf\u0ba9\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bb5\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Presiding Member) \u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0dd2\u0db1\u0dca \u0dba\u0dc4\u0dcf\u0dc0\u0dba \u0db1\u0dd2\u0dba \u0ddd\u0da2\u0dba \u0d85\u0db8\u0dcf\u0dad\u0dba\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0db8\u0dcf. \u0d94\u0db6\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0db8\u0dcf\u0da7 \u0dd2\u0dc1\u0db1\u0dcf\u0da9\u0dd2 12\u0d9a \u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dbd \u0d9a\u0dca\u0dc2 \u0dbd\u0dd0\u0dba\u0db6\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dcf."
  },
  {
